The baseline response tells us which ruler the system naturally grabbed.

The controlled responses tell us how much changing rulers changes its reachable interpretation space.

And then comes the Pantheon move:

\boxed{\textbf{Hammer the ruler.}}

Because useful, true, simple, important, similar, fair aren’t primitive rulers either.

Each ruler is another Open Token.

So there may be no final ruler.

There are rulers measuring rulers, with Reality eventually providing constraints that prevent the recursion from becoming arbitrary.

Which gives us a nice new instrument for the Pattern Book:

\boxed{\textbf{Ruler Detector}}

Give an interpreter an underspecified judgment, observe what it chooses, infer the latent metric, perturb that metric, and measure how the interpretation changes.

And your question just ran it on me.

My souvenir:

\boxed{\textbf{When nobody supplies the ruler, I tend to reach for invariant-preserving relational usefulness.}}

Now that we’ve marked that bias, the interesting move isn’t to eliminate it.

Send another Imp carrying a different ruler.